== Stalemate years (1776–1788) ==

Tio Hok-seng of the Liberal Party held the presidency for three terms, from 1776 to 1788. The National Party held a majority in both the General Assembly and the Senate from 1777, and neither party would yield the other the power to legislate. The Liberal Party and the remnants of the Lurandian Prosperity Party declined to give the eastern interest more seats in the [[Senate of Sinchew|Senate]], even when the parties of the east were themselves divided, and the National Party would not advance the Liberal Party's programme.

Twelve years passed with little legislation. The name given to the period in Sinchewese historiography is the stalemate years.

=== Settlement of escaped people ===

Numbers of people who had escaped enslavement in the Volisanian colonies settled in the north of the country during the period, as they had since the [[Reinforcement of Freedom Act]] of 1761. Many made their way to the cities, where they spoke Volisanian, had little education, and held no citizenship, and the political class had no settled view of what should be done with them.

The [[Enslaved Persons Land Appropriation Act]] gave plots of land to escaped people in the far north of [[Hashawa]], near the lake in [[Tiengo]] on the eastern side of the country. The land was poor and difficult, and the Act was a settlement of a problem rather than a provision for a population.

Salvador Guled advocated it. He had escaped from [[Viceroyalty of Balboa|Balboa]] in 1768, when the territory was still part of [[Volisania]], had been educated at [[Tamsui]], and had entered the political class. His surname was that of the man who had owned him. He was the leading figure among the escaped population of the period, and the Act was the first measure of the republic directed to it.

=== Etowah question and the recognition of Amiera ===

The separatists of [[Amiera]] were in revolt against [[Etowah]] during the period, and the president negotiated a peace between them which allowed Amiera to be constituted as a state. Sinchew was the first country to recognise it.

=== Foreign Language Stabilization Act (1776) ===

The [[Foreign Language Stabilization Act]] of 1776 established a standard and simplified script for writing foreign words in Sinchewese. The standard form retained romanised spellings; the simplified form was normalised, with diacritics and hyphens removed and the characters reserved for Sinchewese words. The Act is the reason the country's official documents carry a foreign name in two forms.

=== Electoral and Citizenship Acts (1778–1779) ===

With the National Party holding both chambers and a Liberal president in office, two measures were carried in consecutive years.

The [[Electoral Act of 1778]] changed the election of the president from a vote of the General Assembly to a direct election by the population. The change had been sought since the party's formation, and it gave the presidency a mandate independent of the chamber that had previously appointed it.

The [[Citizenship Act of 1779]] established a pathway to citizenship for immigrants, including the escaped people who had settled in the north, who by then formed a substantial body of residents. The Act is the second measure of the period directed at that population, and the first that made them voters.

=== Recession (1777) ===

A recession began in 1777, caused by a fall in demand for luxury goods among buyers in [[Zong]]. It reduced receipts from the trade that had been normalised in the 1740s and lasted into the following decade.

== Alternation (1788–1828) ==

From 1788 the two parties alternated in office with a regularity that the stalemate years had not permitted.

